    "rationale": "Iteration 0 raced the baseline on all three development tracks, and every trial ended off-track. max_error sat at about 0.061 m, just past the 0.06 m limit, with one line-loss event per run and rms_error about 0.03. The robot tracks reasonably and then leaves the tape in a single excursion at the first real bend, which points to slow correction rather than poor sensing. The Builder's new geometry (16 mm footprints at 2 cm pitch, mass 0.5 kg, wheel_track 0.10 m) should make yaw response faster, and the controller needs to match it. I checked the sensing by hand. With 16 mm footprints, a centred line reads about 1 on the middle sensor and about 0.03 on each ±0.02 sensor, so the signal is near zero. At a 0.01 m offset it is about 0.5. At 0.03 m it is about 1.5, and beyond about 0.03 m the outer sensor saturates the signal at 2. So the signal is well graded across the working range, and the outer weights of ±2 are not needed to fix a weak near-centre signal as they were with 12 mm footprints. For this geometry I raise kp from 2.0 to 3.0 so the turn command responds more strongly to the graded signal, and I raise kd from 0.05 to 0.15 to damp the faster yaw response and limit overshoot. base_speed drops from 1.2 to 1.1 so the lighter robot takes bends at a speed it can hold the line through. This costs a little time, but the 0.06 m off-track limit is the binding constraint right now. ki stays at 0 to avoid integral wind-up on bends. line_loss stays 'last_direction', which worked as the recovery direction in iteration 0. Expected result: fewer off-track exits and max_error below 0.06 m. Several parameters change together, so attribution will be rough.",
